Certainly! Here is a JavaScript program that utilizes advanced features like Promises, async/await, and destructuring to fetch data from an API, process it, and display the results:

```javascript
 
(async function fetchAndProcessUserData() {
  const fetchJson = async (url) => {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
    return await response.json();
  };

  try {
    const apiUrl = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users';

     
    const users = await fetchJson(apiUrl);

     
    const userNames = users.map(({ id, name, email }) => ({ id, name, email }));

     
    await Promise.all(
      userNames.map(async ({ id, name, email }) => {
        const posts = await fetchJson(`https: 
        
        console.log(`User: ${name}`);
        console.log(`Email: ${email}`);
        console.log('Recent Post Titles:');
        
         
        posts.slice(0, 3).forEach(({ title }) => console.log(`- ${title}`));
      })
    );

  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching or processing data:', error);
  }
})();
```

This code fetches user data from a public API, processes it to extract user names and email addresses, and then retrieves and displays the titles of their most recent posts. It demonstrates the use of async functions, Promise handling, and destructuring assignments in JavaScript.